---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import MandalaTool from "../tools/MandalaTool";
---

<ToolPage tool="mandala">
    <Fragment slot="lead">
        Radial patterns at any size and any symmetry, drawn the way a mandala actually is:
        <strong class="text-slate-300">line work</strong>, with a smaller echo of every motif inside itself. Engrave
        them into a disc, or cut them clean through for a suncatcher — in which case the number that matters is not
        how it looks but how much material is left between the motifs, and that is measured for you in millimetres.
    </Fragment>

    <MandalaTool client:only="react">
        <div slot="fallback" class="flex h-[100dvh] flex-col bg-[#08090d]">
            <div class="h-12 shrink-0 border-b border-white/7"></div>
            <div class="flex min-h-0 flex-1">
                <div class="hidden w-60 shrink-0 border-r border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
                <div class="flex-1"></div>
                <div class="hidden w-76 shrink-0 border-l border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="h-7 shrink-0 border-t border-white/7"></div>
        </div>
    </MandalaTool>

    <section slot="notes" class="relative px-4 pb-24">
        <div class="mx-auto w-[min(72rem,100%)]">
            <div class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Cut, the pattern is the only structure</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Engraved, a mandala cannot go wrong: it is a burn on a disc. Cut through, it is
                    <em>holes</em> in a disc, and holes that meet each other separate the disc into pieces. A mandala
                    that will fall into forty petals on the bed looks exactly like one that will not — same drawing,
                    same colours, and no warning until you lift it.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    So two numbers are worked out from the parameters rather than left to be judged by eye: the
                    <strong class="text-slate-300">web</strong> between one motif and the next at its widest point, and
                    the material <strong class="text-slate-300">between rings</strong>. Those two are the whole
                    structure; everything else is a hole. Under about 2 mm a cut disc is fragile in anything but
                    plywood, and under 1 mm it is not a disc any more.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Line work, and an echo</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Two things separate a mandala from a pattern of blobs, and neither is the shape of the motif.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    The first is that a mandala is <strong class="text-slate-300">outlined</strong>. Every one ever
                    drawn is line work — the shapes are drawn as outlines and only sometimes filled in afterwards — and
                    solid areas are the single thing that makes a generated one look generated. It is also a fraction
                    of the burn. The second is the <strong class="text-slate-300">nested echo</strong>: a smaller copy
                    of each motif inside itself, which is what turns a shape into a motif. Both are on by default and
                    both can be turned off; the echo is engraved only, because a smaller hole inside a hole is a ring
                    of material that falls out on its own.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Eight simple motifs, and seventeen built ones</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    The first eight are not arbitrary. Every guide to drawing a mandala reaches for the same handful —
                    teardrops, pointed petals, dots, triangles, arcs, diamonds — because those are the shapes that
                    survive being repeated thirty times without turning to mush.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    They are also all the same <em>kind</em> of shape, and that was the ceiling. Each is one closed
                    curve, symmetric about its slot and bulging outwards — so it can be a petal, a lens, a rhombus or a
                    slot, and it can never be a star, an arrow or a rosette. A star has
                    <strong class="text-slate-300">two radii</strong>; an arrow is not symmetric end to end; a rosette
                    is seven shapes rather than one. No amount of taste in the curve gets you there.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    So the seventeen below are drawn a different way: in a little coordinate system of their own, as any
                    number of closed rings made of straight runs, arcs and thickened centrelines, and then bent onto
                    the band. Those are the shapes a clip-art mandala kit is full of, and they are what makes a ring
                    read as designed rather than sampled.
                </p>
                <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-4 text-sm leading-relaxed sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Lotus petals</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Drawn out to a sharp point at each end — the shape most people picture when they hear the word.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Petals</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">A lens: widest in the middle, pointed at both ends.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Drops</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Narrow at the hub and round at the rim, like a flame. Reads as motion round the circle.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Diamonds</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Straight sides to a point either side — the one angular motif, and what makes a ring read as geometry rather than flowers.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Darts</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Triangles standing on the hub and widening to the rim. One ring of them on a big hub is a sunburst.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Spokes</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Nearly parallel sides with rounded ends. The most open of the set and the strongest when cut — this is the one for a suncatcher.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Scallops</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Fat almost all the way out, so what is left between them is a thin rib. Handsome engraved and the first to break cut.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Dots</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">A ring of small circles instead of a band of shapes. Structural rather than decorative: without one, a run of bands reads as concentric fences.</dd>
                    </div>
                </dl>

                <h3 class="mt-6 text-sm font-semibold tracking-wide text-slate-300 uppercase">Built from parts</h3>
                <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-4 text-sm leading-relaxed sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Rosettes</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Six petals round a middle — a whole little flower as one motif, rather than one petal repeated. The most ornate of the set, and the one that most obviously could not be a single curve.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Stars</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Five points, the first aimed at the rim. Two radii alternating, which is exactly the thing a petal profile cannot do.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Arrows</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">A shaft with a chevron head and a fletched tail. The only motif that <em>points</em>, so a ring of them reads as rotation rather than as decoration.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Paisley</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">A boteh: round at the bottom, curling to a point, with a dot in the belly. Asymmetric on purpose — the shape that most looks drawn by hand.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Crescents</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Moons, thick in the middle and tapering to a horn at each end. Like the drops, they read as motion round the circle, but from both ends at once.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Chevrons</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">A plain V-bar with rounded corners. The running border every printed mandala has somewhere, and the most open of the built set — the one to reach for when cutting.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Greek key</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">A line that turns four corners. Angular and architectural; one ring of it under a ring of rosettes is the oldest pairing there is.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Squares &amp; hexagons</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Flat sides and corners. The plainest way to make a ring read as architecture rather than as a flower. The hexagons stand on their points and are sized so the shoulders, not the vertices, are what nearly touches the neighbour.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Eight-pointed stars</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Shallower notches than the five-pointed one, so they survive being repeated forty times where the five turns into a blur.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Small circles</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">A circle with another inside it. The plainest motif here and the one that does the most work — a ring of them between two busy bands is what stops the whole disc reading as one texture.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Spirals</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Two and a half turns each. Busy: give them a deep band and a low symmetry, or they close up into discs.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">S-curves</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Two bends the same size in opposite directions. Because alternate rings are already turned half a slot, a band of them reads as a running braid without any two of them touching.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Vines &amp; leaves</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">A stem with leaves alternating along it, swept back towards the root the way a real one grows. The only motif in the set symmetric about nothing at all.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Lattice</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Cross-hatch inside a border, clipped to it. Hatching that runs past its own border is not a lattice, it is a scribble with a box drawn round it.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Line focus</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Parallel lines leaning across the slot. Straight radial ones would line up with the next ring's and read as a fence; the lean makes a band of them read as a twist.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Radiating axes</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">A spike from the inner edge to the outer with a lozenge near its end — the axis a hand-drawn mandala rules in before anything is drawn on it.</dd>
                    </div>
                </dl>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Bands are as tall as what is in them</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    The rings used to split the radius evenly, and that was the single thing that most made a generated
                    mandala look generated. A hand-drawn one is a stack of bands of <em>different</em> heights: a
                    thread of dots, then a deep band of lotus petals, then a narrow line of hatching, then rosettes.
                    Equal bands turn all of that into a set of concentric fences, and no choice of motif rescues it —
                    a ring of dots given a rosette's room is mostly empty space, and the rosettes are cramped by
                    exactly the same measure.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    So each motif asks for the room it needs and the ring heights follow. Dots and hatching take about
                    half a share; rosettes take a third more than one.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Composing a stack by hand</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Almost every control here moves the whole drawing, deliberately. There is one exception, and it
                    earns it: <strong class="text-slate-300">which motif goes in which ring</strong>. A mandala worth
                    cutting is stacked — busy, quiet, busy, quiet — and that is a decision per ring which nothing else
                    can express.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    It stays out of the way. The rings arrive filled in from the seed, so nobody is ever looking at a
                    column of empty dropdowns; change one and the rest freeze exactly as they were, because otherwise
                    nudging the symmetry afterwards would reshuffle everything you had not touched. One button hands
                    them back to the seed.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Two directions of space</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    <strong class="text-slate-300">Between motifs</strong> opens them up sideways, within their own
                    ring, as a share of the slot each one gets.
                    <strong class="text-slate-300">Motif height</strong> opens them up along the radius, by leaving air
                    at the top and bottom of every band. Both are needed: a motif squeezed sideways but still touching
                    the edges of its band reads as cramped whatever the symmetry, and no value of the first reaches
                    that.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Cut, both are material. The figure in the status bar for the space between rings counts the gap
                    between the bands <em>plus</em> the air the motifs leave at their own edges — reporting the gap
                    alone would understate it by the whole of the height control the moment that was turned down.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Why a motif is drawn in its own little world</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    A built motif is authored on a square patch — across the slot one way, out along the band the
                    other, both measured in <strong class="text-slate-300">band heights</strong> — and then bent onto
                    the ring so that a point sits the same number of millimetres round the circle whatever radius it
                    ends up at. The obvious alternative, authoring in the slot's own angular units, makes every shape a
                    different proportion on every ring: a star drawn once would be a starfish on the inner ring and a
                    snowflake on the outer.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    It also means the motifs keep the proportions they were drawn with. When the symmetry gets high
                    enough that two neighbours would touch, a motif is narrowed <em>across</em> the slot and never
                    along it — squeezing both ways would leave a gap at the inner and outer edge of the band, and the
                    ring would stop reading as a ring.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    One consequence is worth knowing about if you cut. The web is worked out over
                    <em>every point</em> of a motif, not just its widest, because the widest point is not where two
                    neighbours come closest: a point eats a fixed number of millimetres of the circle whatever radius
                    it is at, so the angular room it takes grows as the radius shrinks. An arrow's fletching is
                    narrower than its head and much further in, and it is the fletching that decides how close two
                    arrows get.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-2">
                <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Every ring is turned half a slot</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Rings whose motifs sat at the same angles would read as spokes running all the way from the hub
                        to the rim, which is a wheel rather than a mandala. Every other ring is offset by half its
                        slot, so the pattern reads as a weave — and it puts material where the neighbouring ring has a
                        hole, which is also why a cut one holds.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Few controls, on purpose</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        There is no slider per ring. That would be more powerful and would produce worse mandalas: what
                        makes one look deliberate is that everything in it follows from the same two or three
                        decisions. Symmetry, rings and the web move the whole drawing at once, and the
                        <em>mixed</em> motif picks a different shape per ring from a seed you can go back to.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="reveal mt-5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-500">
                Every dimension is a fraction of the diameter, so the pattern is the same shape at 40 mm and at 400 —
                but the web is not, because it is a fraction of a circle that got smaller. A design that survives being
                cut at 200 mm can fall apart at 80.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</ToolPage>
